  "configuration_id": "config_001",
  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so there is zero information about which modality, encoder, profile setting, or fusion strategy is most effective. The most critical uncertainty is establishing a performance baseline against which all other design choices (multiprofile, learned encoders, image modality, multimodal fusion) can be meaningfully compared. 2) What this tests: config_001 (rna_only, singleprofile, pca_n2000) establishes the simplest and most standard RNA-only baseline. PCA with 2000 components is a neutral, fast, non-learned representation; singleprofile avoids aggregation confounds. This gives a clean performance anchor. 3) Why it is the best next experiment: With a limited budget, the first experiment should be fast to run (PCA is computationally cheap, no image encoder, no aggregation, no fusion) so we get a reliable signal quickly. It directly enables the next logical comparisons: (a) multiprofile RNA with aggregation (configs 002/003) to isolate the effect of aggregation, (b) learned RNA encoders (configs 004-009) to isolate the effect of representation, (c) image-only baselines (configs 010-015) to compare modalities, and (d) multimodal fusion (configs 016+) to test whether integration adds value over the best unimodal result. Starting here avoids confounding multiple design changes in the first data point and gives the strongest interpretive foundation for all subsequent experiments."
}

